WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.760
- In 1986, the residents
of New High Street

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.200
in Headington, near Oxford,

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.420
woke up to find there was
a new addition to the road:

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.100
a shark in the roof of number two.

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.450
It was commissioned by
the owner of the house.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.130
It's protest artwork, anti-war,

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.680
symbolic of bombs crashing
down into buildings.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:16.020
And of course, English planning
laws do not allow someone

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:18.000
to suddenly install a
giant shark sculpture

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:19.860
in their roof
in a residential area.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.440
At least, not without
consulting the neighbors first.

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.580
- So in 1986, my dad put a 25ft fiberglass and steel shark

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:27.810
through the roof of his house.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:30.330
He'd just bought his first house

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:33.990
and the first night he moved in,
he heard planes going overhead,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.310
he was sort of woken by this.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:39.090
And in the morning
he read the newspaper

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:44.090
and, I suppose, saw destroyed houses
in Libya that had been bombed.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:47.670
The story goes that my dad
and sculptor John Buckley

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.690
were sat, drinking, celebrating,
the purchase of this house,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:57.630
sort of mulling over how to deal with
the shock of the bombing of Tripoli.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:01:00.900
They were up for a fight,
and they did this shark

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:02.910
without planning permission, in secret.

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.680
There was this sort of mixed reaction.

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.300
A lot of people were shocked

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.920
and didn't quite know
what was going on.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.110
I think some people thought
it might have been real

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:13.710
which is an eyeopener and
certainly funny looking back.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:15.810
A lot of people just loved it
and thought it was brilliant,

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:17.460
right from the word go.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.230
The council, obviously, were
very much "not here, thank you,

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.200
"and certainly not without
planning permission, this is outrageous".

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.390
- So the months and years
after the shark was installed

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:29.490
played out exactly as you would expect.

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:33.233
The council said take it down.
The owner said no. It went to court.

00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:36.360
It took six years to resolve and the shark
was eventually allowed to stay

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.820
when a senior British
government official intervened

00:01:38.820 --> 00:01:42.210
and said, "Yes, this has
enough artistic merit that,

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:44.310
"as a one off, it can stay."

00:01:44.310 --> 00:01:46.470
And now it's an icon of the area,
known around the world,

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:47.820
and as far as I can tell,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:50.070
generally liked by the folks around here.

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.140
Perhaps a bit more than the
current owner would like.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.180
Before we get to that though,
I did have a couple

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.040
of more practical
questions about the shark.

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:57.900
Like: does it have a head?

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:58.980
- It doesn't have a head.

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:02.280
It's just a half bust of a
shark crashing through the roof.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.370
I think they cut a crude
hole and dropped that in

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.472
with a crane initially,

00:02:07.472 --> 00:02:11.190
and then sort of shored it up
as soon as they humanly could.

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:14.820
There was no preparation, obviously,
because that would've tipped people off.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.310
It's fiberglass with steel reinforcement.

00:02:17.310 --> 00:02:20.340
The steel struts on the inside connect out

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.500
through the ceiling and
down into the walls.

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.550
So it's structurally connected.
It's there to stay.

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.780
It needs repainting every now and then.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.900
And there's just continual
checking to make sure

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.780
everything's in tiptop condition.

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:35.070
- This year, thanks for a nomination

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:37.170
from a presumably well-meaning
member of the public,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.650
the shark was put on the
local list of heritage assets.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:43.110
Now the shark isn't on the national list
of protected buildings yet.

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:45.630
It could still be taken down
without breaking the law,

00:02:45.630 --> 00:02:48.420
but local registration
is often the first step

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:50.190
to being on the national list.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:53.220
And owning a listed building
is a pretty significant burden.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.004
The local government requires you
to keep it in good condition

00:02:56.004 --> 00:02:58.033
and also keep it the same.

00:02:58.033 --> 00:03:01.028
And if you don't, they can fix
it themselves and bill you.

00:03:01.028 --> 00:03:03.650
Art, just like anything
else, requires upkeep.

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:05.908
And there are news stories that show that over the last few decades,

00:03:05.908 --> 00:03:09.630
the shark, and this house, were
sometimes in extremely poor condition.

00:03:09.630 --> 00:03:11.430
- Drop a shark through
the roof of your house,

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:15.690
there's some leakage problems that
one might immediately encounter!

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:18.090
It turns out it's very
difficult to repair those.

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:21.060
Until finally my mum ultimately stepped in

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:24.660
and did the whole thing up to, I think,
quite a nice standard.

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.240
Though I've been renting
it out as an Airbnb,

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:30.120
it's just a lovely place
and it means a lot to me

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:35.250
and I like that people can come and be
close to it and part of it too.

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:38.850
So someone put it forward for the
Oxford Asset Heritage Register.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:42.840
On the council website, they say that
there's absolutely no restrictions placed.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.030
This is just for their record keeping.

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:48.690
But they do mention that any
planning permission changes

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:51.411
will necessarily take
this into consideration.

00:03:51.411 --> 00:03:55.470
I thought this was an
interesting take because, well,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.320
nominally this is here
to preserve this shark

00:03:58.320 --> 00:04:01.140
but this is controlled
through the planning laws.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.330
And seems to me you can't
really preserve something

00:04:04.330 --> 00:04:07.800
that's specifically
there to challenge that.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.390
That seems to be completely absurd.
And to miss sort of the artistic point,

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:14.940
which is probably the thing
you would want to preserve,

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:16.710
if you're preserving a piece of art!

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:19.020
- As a bystander, I love art like this,

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:20.970
where part of the work
is how it interfaces

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:22.650
with the world and with bureaucracy.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.080
But for the local officials
it must be frustrating

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.780
because they know that
anything they do to this shark

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.210
becomes part of the art,
part of the story.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.310
List it, unlist it, demand its removal,

00:04:32.310 --> 00:04:34.260
demand its preservation,
deliberately ignore it,

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:36.678
whatever they do, that's art now!

00:04:36.678 --> 00:04:38.610
I wouldn't want to be in that position.

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.590
- So my dad passed away in 2019.

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:43.710
We spoke a little bit about
the listing just before he did.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.120
He had sort of mixed feelings.

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:47.280
On the one hand, he had
been fighting

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.459
against the council for
a large portion of his life.

00:04:50.459 --> 00:04:53.610
And on the other hand, he
had these considerations

00:04:53.610 --> 00:04:56.010
about the legacy of the
place moving forward

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:58.890
and liked that they
were acknowledging him.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.809
You've got this idea that
the council have come around

00:05:01.809 --> 00:05:04.980
and changed their mind
and are now embracing

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:06.818
something they weren't before.

00:05:06.818 --> 00:05:10.710
And on the other hand, you've got this,
I think, slightly more truthful side,

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:12.750
which is that these are
totally different councillors.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:15.129
The councillors that hated it
are all gone.

00:05:15.129 --> 00:05:18.120
And these new ones,
although they love it

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.280
are applying the same kind of control,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.760
which again, flies in
the face of the piece.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:25.770
I guess there's a bit of conflict there.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:29.610
I wish he was still here to ask
and see how it all panned out.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.630
I think he'd like that we're still
talking about it today, very much.

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.810
- For now, the shark is not
protected, not officially.

00:05:36.810 --> 00:05:38.823
In a few years... who knows?

